# Commercial S10.2C cutover guard recovery — 2026-09-04

## Safe observation

The authorized S10.2B/S10.2C controller preflight completed GREEN against
Control commit `b0808b349c5d8c4cb2203c191f1800dc4442343a`. The first deployment attempt
then stopped fail-closed at `S8_COPY_DRIFT`. Its automatic rollback returned
the Application repository to source commit
`deeb38c30427066989eb85e1c115d2aeccf140cf`; Application and Control remained
clean, the public S7/S8/S10/nginx services were active with zero restarts, and
the public SFW endpoints returned their expected successful status codes.

The rollback's retired S8 one-shot health gate subsequently returned
`S8_LANDING_DEEP_LINK_MISSING`. This did not stop a recurring health timer: the
S8 health timer is intentionally retired, while S9 and S10 remain the recurring
health authorities.

No token, message, media, user identifier or other personal data is recorded in
this evidence. Adult media, AVS, payments and publishing remained closed.

## Root cause

The controller used the source S8 and S10 copy hashes for both the source and
target Application commits, although the reviewed target intentionally contains
the English-first WMS copy. The target files therefore could never pass the
post-install hash gate.

The versioned S8 one-shot health wrapper also still searched the landing HTML
for the historical direct Telegram deep link. The current versioned product
loop exposes an internal, allowlisted tracking route and redirects that route
to the bot identity from the active S8 contract. The wrapper therefore rejected
the current source landing after rollback even though its service and public
endpoint were healthy.

## Repair and rollback plan

1. Bind source and target S8/S10 copy files to separate exact hashes.
2. Make the existing S8 health wrapper validate the internal tracked route and
   its redirect against the active contract's bot identity.
3. Install and compare that wrapper through the existing S10.2B controller
   before quiescing, so every subsequent failure can use the compatible guard.
4. Extend the existing backup to archive the pre-cutover installed wrapper.
   Keep the repaired, source-compatible wrapper during automatic rollback; the
   archive permits an exact manual executable restore if separately required.
5. Add regression tests for both target copy hashes, the current bot redirect
   and rejection of a legacy-bot redirect.
6. Keep deployment disabled until a replacement backup made by the extended
   versioned backup script is verified and recorded.

No second deployment attempt is authorized from an old backup that lacks the
runtime-executable archive. The first extended backup was safely rejected by
the controller before mutation because its descriptive suffix was outside the
existing exact deployment-name allowlist. Replacement backup
`20260904T114648Z-pre-s10-2b-public-telegram` was then created by the extended
versioned script with the allowlisted name and verified before deployment.
